DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The lloogg(), llooggff(), and llooggll() functions compute the natural logarithm of
     _x.

     The lloogg1100(), lloogg1100ff(), and lloogg1100ll() functions compute the logarithm base
     10 of _x, while lloogg22(), lloogg22ff(), and lloogg22ll() compute the logarithm base 2
     of _x.

     The lloogg11pp(), lloogg11ppff(), and lloogg11ppll() functions compute the natural loga‐
     rithm of 1 + x.  Computing the natural logarithm as log1p(x) is more ac‐
     curate than computing it as log(1 + x) when _x is close to zero.

RREETTUURRNN VVAALLUUEESS
     These functions return the requested logarithm; the logarithm of 1 is +0.
     An attempt to take the logarithm of ±0 results in a divide-by-zero excep‐
     tion, and -∞ is returned.  Otherwise, attempting to take the logarithm of
     a negative number results in an invalid exception and a return value of
     _N_a_N.

